Current state, measured (scripts/one-off survey, not committed):

  group        dir                            active  ignored  roms   in json
  cpu          test_roms/cpu                      39        0    39        39
  ppu          test_roms/ppu                      41        1    42        42
  apu          test_roms/apu                      46       18    64        46
  input        test_roms/input                     2        2     4         4
  m004_txrom   test_roms/mapper/m004_txrom         7        0     7         7
  m005_exram   test_roms/mapper/m005_exrom         2        0     2         2
                                              -------  -------
                                                  137       21

21 ignored, and they are NOT one problem - they are four, in increasing
difficulty. The 20 non-PPU ones have no tests.json entry at all, so there is
nothing to compare against; only ppu::vbl_nmi_even_odd_timing has an
expectation and genuinely fails.

  After 7b: 145 active / 13 ignored (apu 54/10).
  After 7f: +141 mapper unit tests (not ROM tests), 64 then 77.
  After 7c: 153 active / 5 ignored - apu 62/2 (phase_reset, volumes),
            ppu 41/1 (vbl_nmi_even_odd_timing), input 2/2 (zapper).

7a. BLOCKER, fix first: `audio` is unassertable-by-accident. -- DONE (with 7b)
    TestFrame.audio and RomTest.audio were both #[serde(skip_serializing)]. They
    deserialize fine, so a hand-written audio test works - but UPDATE_SNAPSHOT=1
    rewrote tests.json WITHOUT them, silently converting every audio test into
    a frame-buffer test. Now skip_serializing_if = "is_false", so `true`
    survives a rewrite and `false` still stays out of the file. Verified by
    forcing a hash mismatch on an audio:true entry and confirming both the
    RomTest and TestFrame flags came back. Landing a real audio test to prove
    the hash path end to end is still open - it belongs to 7c.

7b. Cheapest real win: mechanical result assertions.  -- DONE, [8 tests]
    Two mechanisms, because the notes' premise was wrong about half of them:

    - $6000 status protocol (4): apu::{dmc,noise,square,triangle}, the apu_mixer
      set. They write $DE $B0 $61 to $6001-$6003 and a code to $6000 ($80
      running, $81 needs reset, $00-$7F done, 0 = pass), plus NUL-terminated
      text at $6004 which now goes into the failure message. New
      TestFrame.status; `number` is a BUDGET not a target - the loop stops as
      soon as the ROM reports, so a pass costs ~1-2s instead of the full cap.
      Read via Bus::peek (honours whatever the board maps at $6000) rather than
      Src::PrgRam directly; ControlDeck::wram is $0000-$07FF and never reaches
      it. The marker check is load-bearing: PRG-RAM powers on as zeros, which
      reads as "finished, code 0, passed" before the ROM runs an instruction.

    - report tones (4): the whole dmc_tests set - dmc_buffer_retained,
      dmc_latency, dmc_status, dmc_status_irq. These do NOT use $6000. Measured:
      they never write PRG-RAM at all, render an all-zero blank screen with
      rendering off, leave nothing in WRAM but stack remnants, and end in an
      identical `STA $4015 / LDA #$09 / STA $4003 / JMP *` epilogue. They report
      ONLY audibly. Per blargg's readme.txt: the code in binary, one tone per
      bit, leading zeroes skipped, always a leading zero tone - so a pass (code
      0) is exactly ONE tone and any failure is two or more. New
      TestFrame.tones counts pulse-1 bursts, which tells pass from fail without
      needing to know which pitch is which and without hashing a single sample.
      dmc_latency was filed under 7c ("compare output"); it is one of these.

    Neither expectation is ever rewritten by UPDATE_SNAPSHOT - they are
    hand-written and mechanically meaningful, so a mismatch is a bug to fix.
    All four failure paths were negative-tested (wrong code, still-running,
    no marker, wrong tone count) and produce readable messages.
    $81/needs-reset is deliberately NOT handled implicitly: it panics telling
    you to add an Action::Reset frame, since tests.json can already express
    that.

7c. Audio-waveform tests. -- 8 of 10 DONE (was 11; dmc_latency went to 7b)
    Enabled: apu_env, dmc_pitch, lin_ctr, noise_pitch, square_pitch,
    sweep_cutoff, sweep_sub, triangle_pitch. NOT enabled: phase_reset and
    volumes, whose #[ignore] said "unknown" - nobody has established what they
    should sound like, so a snapshot would just bless whatever we do today.
    Their ignore reason now says that explicitly.

    DECISION (this was the open question): NOT a raw-sample hash. New
    TestFrame.audio_profile records one (rms, crossing_rate) pair per 1/16th
    of the run:
      - RMS about the bucket's mean, in 1/10000 full scale - the envelope,
        about the mean so a DC offset does not move it.
      - Mean-crossing rate in Hz - also DC-offset invariant, and per SECOND
        rather than per sample so changing the sample rate does not move it.
    Compared with a tolerance (+/-5 each) so float drift needs no re-snapshot.
    Seed with "audio_profile": [] in tests.json + UPDATE_SNAPSHOT=1.

    The crossing rate is a TIMBRE FINGERPRINT, NOT a fundamental-frequency
    estimate - on a filtered harmonic waveform it tracks high-frequency content
    and can move opposite to the fundamental. Do not read it as pitch. So this
    is a change detector that names the bucket that moved, not a correctness
    oracle.

    CALIBRATED, not guessed: perturbing the pulse timer period by 2 is caught
    by all four pulse-driven tests (apu_env, square_pitch, sweep_cutoff,
    sweep_sub) and by none of the four driving other channels - no misses, no
    false positives. Which channel each ROM drives was measured, not assumed:
    apu_env/square_pitch/sweep_sub/sweep_cutoff = pulse1, lin_ctr/triangle_pitch
    = triangle, noise_pitch = noise, dmc_pitch = DMC. An earlier 8-bucket,
    coarser-quantised version missed 2 of the 4 - hence 16 buckets and 1/10000.

    NB the tone-count trick from 7b does NOT transfer: these ROMs are demos
    whose audio is the test content, not a report, so their burst counts
    (measured: 0, 1 or 2 depending on ROM) mean nothing.
    RISK: an audio hash is far more brittle than a frame hash - it pins every
    sample, so any filter/mixer/sample-rate change churns all 11. Decide up
    front whether to hash raw samples or something coarser (e.g. per-channel
    RMS, or a downsampled envelope) that tests pitch/envelope behavior without
    pinning the exact filter output. Recommend coarser; revisit if it misses
    real regressions. The two "unknown" ones need their expected behavior
    established by hand first - do them last, and do not snapshot a wrong
    result into tests.json.

7d. The "genuine failure". -- DONE, and it was NOT a failure. [1 test]
    ppu::vbl_nmi_even_odd_timing was ignored as "clock is skipped too late
    relative to enabling BG, Failed #3". It passes. The ROM reports $6000 code
    0 and prints "10-even_odd_timing / Passed".

    Whatever that reason described was fixed at some point and never
    re-checked, because the test was ignored AND its expectation was a
    placeholder `hash: 0` - a value no real frame ever hashes to, so it could
    not have passed even once the bug went away. Converting it to a $6000
    status assertion (7b's mechanism) surfaced this immediately.

    LESSON: `hash: 0` is a placeholder, not an expectation, and it hides
    exactly this. Worth grepping for others - and the rest of the blargg-based
    ppu/cpu tests are still frame hashes when they could be status assertions
    with real messages. That is a cheap follow-up.

7e. Zapper. -- DONE, both, plus a vacuous existing test made real. [4 green]
    The existing Action enum already covered all of it (ToggleZapperConnected,
    ZapperAim, ZapperTrigger are all handled in on_frame_action), so no harness
    work was needed there.

    KEY FACT (from Luke, and it is the whole puzzle): EACH OF THESE ROMS
    REPORTS THROUGH A DIFFERENT CHANNEL, AND NONE RESPOND TO AIM ALONE. That is
    why every "aim somewhere and hash the screen" approach came up empty, and
    it is what made an earlier read of this - "their output does not depend on
    the zapper" - WRONG. It depends on the trigger, or on the audio, or both.

      - zapper_light reports BY SOUND: audible while the aim is over the white
        region, silent off it, and audible constantly when unplugged. Measured
        RMS 0.055 vs 0.002, a 28x split. Asserts 7c's audio_profile: loud
        buckets 0-7 (aimed at the white box), silent 8-15 (aimed off it).
      - zapper_trigger reports BY SOUND on the trigger, and does NOT depend on
        aim - it is a trigger test, so it beeps wherever it is pointed. Also an
        audio_profile: quiet, then a spike right after the trigger.
      - zapper_stream reports ON SCREEN, but only once triggered: it prints `0`
        per read until the trigger and `1` while it is held. Eyeballed both
        frames before snapshotting - all `0`s at frame 15, a run of `111111` at
        frame 30. Two frame hashes.

    zapper_light was previously ENABLED AND VACUOUS: all four of its frames
    asserted the same hash of the same static screen, so it passed with the
    zapper unplugged. It now asserts something.

    Negative-tested, and the failures land exactly where they should: making
    the trigger never assert fails 3 of 4 (flip, stream, trigger); making
    light_sense always report darkness fails only zapper_light. Note that
    merely RAISING the light threshold from 85 to 250 fails nothing - the white
    region is brighter than both - so this tests light-vs-dark, not the
    threshold itself.

7f. Missing coverage -- DONE, every board in the table now has a group
    [64 tests for the 8 high-risk boards, then 77 more for the last 13]
    22 boards in the boards! table; exactly 2 had a test group (m004, m005),
    and the mapper rework touched all of them.

    NOT done with ROMs. christopherpow/nes-test-roms has almost no mapper
    coverage beyond the mmc3_*/mmc5test/exram dirs we already use - there is no
    holydiverbatman-style per-board set in it - and the useful ROMs for these
    boards do not exist publicly at all (nobody has written a VRC6 or N163 IRQ
    test ROM). So this went to in-crate unit tests instead, which is the better
    tool anyway: the rework changed the *read path* (Banks -> page tables), not
    the per-board bank arithmetic, so a page-table assertion pins exactly what
    moved and names what broke, where a frame hash would only say "different".

    mapper::test_utils (factored out of the MMC5 test module) builds a cart
    with every 1K page filled with its own index - PRG page n = n, CHR page n =
    0x80|n, PRG-RAM = 0x5A - so a peek names the bank it came from and a test
    reads as "writing $8000 = 3 puts PRG page 3 at $8000". No ROM needed. Plus
    write/prg_peek/chr_peek that mirror Bus::write / Bus::peek / Ppu::chr_peek
    routing.

    Covered: VrcIrq (7), Vrc6 (12), Namco163 (10), SunsoftFme7 (8), Sxrom/MMC1
    (8), JalecoSs88006 (8), BandaiFCG (8), Fk23C (10). Every board also gets an
    update_banks_rebuilds_every_window_from_register_state test, since that is
    what Ppu::rebuild_mapper_state relies on after a save state - page tables
    are never serialized.

    THREE BUGS FOUND, all in VRC6, all latent (i.e. reachable by legal register
    values, would panic a debug build, but not hit by Akumajou Densetsu / Esper
    Dream 2 / Mouryou Senki Madara in 900 frames - measured: saw rate peaks at
    25, none use IRQ cycle mode):
      - VrcIrq::clock ran the prescaler in cycle mode too, netting +338/clock,
        overflowing the i16 after ~97 CPU cycles.
      - The VRC6 saw's 8-bit accumulator overflowed at any rate above 42; the
        hardware discards the carry, so it now wraps.
      - VrcIrq::reset cleared every field EXCEPT irq_pending, leaving the line
        asserted with nothing able to reach the acknowledge that clears it.
    The other 45 tests passed against the reworked code unchanged, so those
    seven boards' page-table wiring is sound.

    THE REMAINING 13, done second [77 tests]: Nrom (5), Uxrom (6), Cnrom (6),
    Axrom (5), Pxrom/MMC2 (7), Fxrom/MMC4 (7), ColorDreams (5), Bnrom (5),
    Nina001 (6), Gxrom (4), Bf909x (7), Nina003006 (4), NesEvent (10). NO BUGS
    FOUND - all 77 passed against the reworked code unchanged, which is the
    result to expect for boards this simple and is why they were done last.

    What was worth writing anyway, i.e. what is not just "bank n lands at
    $8000":
      - MMC2 vs MMC4 latches. The one documented difference between the two
        boards is that MMC2's LOW half triggers on exactly $0FD8/$0FE8 while
        MMC4 takes the whole $0FD8-$0FDF/$0FE8-$0FEF run; both take the run in
        the high half. Each board's test asserts its own decode AND that the
        other's addresses do nothing to it. A frame hash cannot see this - it
        only shows on the tile either side of the trigger.
      - NesEvent. The lock is a three-state machine that opens on a falling
        then rising timer-control bit and never shuts again, and `load` and
        `reset` leave it in DIFFERENT states (load -> one write from open,
        reset -> needs the full sequence), so the helper loads then resets the
        way ControlDeck::load_rom does. The tournament timer fires at
        0x28000000 CPU cycles for the shipped dip switches, ~6:15 of NTSC play,
        which is asserted as seconds rather than as a magic number. It is
        tested by setting `value` directly - clocking 671M times is not a test.
      - Nina001's registers sit ON TOP of PRG-RAM at $7FFD-$7FFF, so a write
        both banks and stays readable; asserted, since a board that consumed
        the write would break saves.
      - Bf909x's $9000 heuristic: the write that identifies a BF9097 must also
        take effect as mirroring rather than as a bank.

    NEGATIVE-TESTED, 24 mutations, one per behaviour worth pinning (wrong
    window size, swapped nibbles, dropped mask, inverted polarity, widened
    decode, latch ranges swapped between MMC2 and MMC4, lock forced open,
    timer left running). 21 failed exactly the tests that name them. The four
    that did NOT fail anything first time are the useful part:
      - UxROM's bank register masked to 3 bits, and Nina001's PRG register
        left unmasked, were both invisible because the test cart had exactly
        as many banks as the mask allows - masking and wrapping are the same
        map. Both carts are now one size bigger so the width is observable.
      - Timer::reset dropping `started` is invisible through the board, since
        reset's update_state() stops the timer anyway. Asserted on Timer
        directly instead.
      - AxROM's `val & 0x0F` is NOT observable at all: no AxROM has more than
        16 32K banks, and below that masking and wrapping agree. Left
        untested with a comment saying so, rather than faking a 24-bank cart.

    Two divergences from other emulators found while writing these, NEITHER
    changed - no ROM here to settle either, so they are recorded at the test
    that pins current behaviour:
      - Bf909x mirroring polarity. This board is a port of Mesen's BF909x and
        matches it exactly, including `(value & 0x10) ? ScreenA : ScreenB`.
        FCEUX is the other way round (`MI_0 + ((V >> 4) & 1)`), and the wiki
        says only that bit 4 "selects the 1 KiB CIRAM bank". Only Fire Hawk
        cares. Kept Mesen's.
      - Mapper 144's bus conflict. We OR in the ROM's bit 0; the wiki gives
        `EffectiveData = ROM[addr] & (WrittenData | 1)`, which ANDs every bit.
        The two agree whenever a game writes a value to an address holding
        that same value, which is what a cart with bus conflicts must do, so
        the difference only shows on Death Race doing something unusual.

    Follow-up noticed, NOT changed: JalecoSs88006 stores the IRQ reload
    nibbles as full bytes (`irq_reload[i] = val`) and then ORs them shifted by
    4*i, so a write above $0F bleeds into the neighbouring nibble. Real
    SS88006 latches 4 bits. One-word fix (`val & 0x0F`) but no way to validate
    it here, so it is left alone deliberately rather than guessed at.
    Also pre-existing: `items after a test module` clippy warning in
    m005_exrom.rs (impl Debug for Exrom sits after mod tests). Only shows under
    --all-targets, which `cargo make lint` does not pass.

Suggested order: 7a -> 7b -> 7f (a few high-risk boards) -> 7c -> 7e -> 7d.
7a and 7b together turn 7 of 21 ignores green for little work and make the
audio path trustworthy; 7f is where the mapper rework actually carries risk.

================================================================================
PHASE 8 - deferred API work                                (8b/8c DONE, 8a/8d open)
================================================================================

8a. video::Frame holds Box<[u8; Frame::SIZE]> -- DONE.
    Deref/DerefMut<Target = Vec<u8>> are gone, and with them push/clear/
    truncate; the size is now part of the type, so as_array/as_array_mut are
    plain borrows instead of a try_into().expect() on every call. Replaced by
    len(), as_slice/as_mut_slice, AsRef/AsMut<[u8]> and a generic
    Index/IndexMut<I: SliceIndex<[u8]>> - slicing is what the renderer's
    overscan trim needs and it cannot resize anything, which is the whole point.
    One expect() remains, in the constructor, on a Vec allocated at exactly
    SIZE two lines above it.
    Only ONE call site changed: renderer.rs's `&frame_buffer` no longer
    multi-step-coerces (Ref -> Frame -> Vec -> slice) and is now .as_slice().
    The overscan slice and control_deck's `&frame[..]` still work through Index.

    BOTH TRAPS WERE REAL:
      - `Box::new([0u8; SIZE])` would put 240 KiB on the stack. Built through
        `vec![0; SIZE].into_boxed_slice()` instead.
      - Frame::new's alpha = 255 is load-bearing, and this was CONFIRMED by
        reading the filters rather than assumed: decode_buffer and
        apply_ntsc_filter both write pixels[0..=2] and never index 3, so alpha
        is written exactly once, at construction. A zeroed array would have
        rendered every frame fully transparent with every frame-hash test still
        green, since the hashes cover the same RGB bytes the filters write.
    Now asserted by video::tests::a_new_frame_is_opaque_black, plus
    a_frame_can_be_sliced_but_not_resized for the Index path.
    Verified end-to-end, not just by the type checker: rendering spritecans.nes
    to a PNG through the screenshot utility gives byte-identical output before
    and after, alpha 255 throughout.
    NOT DONE: the two public types named `Frame` (video::Frame,
    ppu::frame::Frame) are still both called Frame. Left alone - renaming
    either is churn on an already large branch, and 8d has to describe it.

8b. #![warn(missing_docs)] on tetanes-core -- DONE.
    The draft guessed ~400 items. Measured by turning the lint on: 1,118.
    479 struct fields, 253 methods, 190 variants, 67 assoc fns, 50 constants,
    79 other. By area: mapper 385, apu 236, ppu 167, cpu 123, input 63,
    cart 48, fs 27, bus 15, memory 11, video 10, genie 10, common 9, rest 14.
    Concentrations tell the story - cpu/instr.rs alone is 95 variants (the
    opcode and addressing-mode tables), m005_exrom.rs is 51 fields + 16
    variants. Those are not API; they are the internals the crate publishes.

    So: file-level #![allow(missing_docs)] on the internal wiring, each with a
    comment saying why - every board module, apu/*, ppu/* and cpu/instr.rs -
    and real docs for the ~150 items of genuine API that were left. That is
    ppu.rs's register accessors and its addr/size/cycle/scanline constant
    modules, all of input.rs, cart.rs, fs.rs, genie.rs, common.rs, memory.rs,
    video.rs, debug.rs, bus.rs and the two fs backends.

    NB the WASM fs backend was missing all 7 of its items and only the
    wasm doc job sees it - `cargo make docs` runs docs-web first, which is the
    only thing that catches this. Exactly the omission CLAUDE.md warns about.
    Verified: cargo make lint, check-fmt and docs all clean, 396 tests and 14
    doctests pass, and clippy is clean on nightly, stable AND 1.88.

8c. Audit pub fields on Ppu/Bus/Cpu/Video -- DONE, and the answer was to NOT
    demote anything. The measurement is the useful part.

    MEASURED EXACTLY, not grepped: demote every field on Ppu/Cpu/Bus/Video/
    Apu/Input to pub(crate), then iterate with the compiler until the workspace
    builds again (tetanes + tetanes-utils, --all-targets, and the 16 doctests,
    which link the crate externally and so count as consumers).
    12 of 105 fields survive; 93 demote with zero call-site changes:
      Ppu    8/52: ctrl cycle scanline scroll prerender_scanline
                   vblank_scanline frame memory
      Cpu    2/17: bus pc
      Bus    2/ 8: ppu input
      Video  0/ 2, Apu 0/21, Input 0/5
    And the 12 are three consumers, not a scattered mess: the PPU debugger
    (which destructures a Ppu copy it already owns), rewind swapping
    ppu.frame.buffer, and chrdump reading ppu.memory - plus cpu.bus/bus.ppu as
    pure navigation to reach them.
    Two gotchas if this is ever re-run: Cpu::bus being private masks every
    error behind it, so it takes several rounds; and E0451 reports struct-
    literal fields in the PLURAL ("fields `a`, `b` ... are private"), which a
    regex written for E0616 silently misses.

    WHY NOT DEMOTE, per Luke: demoting 93 leaves Ppu with 8 pub and 44 private
    fields, which is exactly the clippy::partial_pub_fields smell, in exchange
    for an encapsulation that Cpu::bus being pub defeats anyway. ControlDeck
    already shows the shape of that bargain - private fields, but cpu_mut()
    hands out the whole tree, so the privacy buys a `()`. And once 8b allows
    the internals, demoting no longer shrinks the doc surface either, which was
    the original justification for doing 8c first.
    DONE INSTEAD: a "# Stability" section on ppu/cpu/bus/apu/input saying these
    fields are internal wiring exposed for embedders and debuggers and track
    the implementation rather than the crate version, and *derived* marked on
    the four cache fields that desynchronize the emulator if written -
    mapper_ops and the three is_*_scanline flags.

    IF the surface is ever to shrink for real, fields are the wrong lever:
    385 of the 1,118 are in mapper, where every board struct is public only
    because boards! generates a `pub use`. Not re-exporting the board types
    removes a third of the API surface in one edit and nothing outside the
    crate names them.

8d. Breaking changes marked in the commits, not in a hand-written section
    -- DONE, and done by rewording history rather than by prose, per Luke.

    The premise held: NONE of the 63 commits was marked `!` or carried a
    BREAKING CHANGE footer, so the generated 0.15.0 entry would have listed
    every break as an ordinary feat/refactor.

    WHICH commits break was decided MECHANICALLY, not from memory. Enumerating
    every `pub` item in tetanes-core at main and at HEAD and diffing gives the
    NET removals - which is the right question, since two commits on the branch
    (bd60ebc, fb05c4d) change `fs::load_bytes`'s signature and then change it
    back, and marking either would be a lie. 11 commits, plus the video::Frame
    one already marked when it landed:
      f8910db MMC3 page tables       - Mmc3::set_chr_banks
      87c8753 MMC5 page tables       - six Exrom helpers, Cart::chr_size
      05e98d2 delete old memory path - Banks, BankAccess, CIRam, mem module,
                                       Cart::prg_rom/chr_rom, Ppu::ciram
      5a2a83e MapperOps              - three Map methods -> Map::mapper_ops
      c946cdd Map trait diet         - supertraits dropped
      746165b stable board ids       - Fk23C unboxed, Unimplemented is an error
      de06bc3 box SunsoftFme7        - variant boxed, ids renumbered
      5a403f8 four traits deleted
      7c677a7 five traits deleted
      42089d6 ROM out of save states - Cpu::load/load_cpu now fallible
      0159c7b clocking API           - MSRV 1.88, renames, sized frame buffers
    Note `f40c729`'s 22 `pub mod` "removals" are NOT a break: the boards! macro
    regenerates every one of them.

    Rewritten with three `git filter-branch --msg-filter` passes (the branch has
    no upstream, so nothing needed force-pushing). Every pass verified with
    `git diff backup-before-8d HEAD` = empty, i.e. the trees never moved.

    TWO THINGS THAT BIT, both worth knowing before doing this again:
      - Rewriting hashes STRANDS the hashes quoted inside other commit bodies.
        Four commits referenced earlier ones by short hash and would have
        pointed at objects no longer reachable. Fixed with a second pass using
        filter-branch's own `map` shell function, which resolves old -> new
        *during* the rewrite; a naive two-step map is wrong, because pass 2
        changes the hashes again. The same applies to this notes file, whose
        seven references were remapped by position (verified tree-identical).
      - git-conventional TRUNCATES a BREAKING CHANGE footer at any continuation
        line that begins with a backticked `::` path - `Map::sync` at the start
        of a line reads as a new footer token. Seven of the eleven footers were
        silently cut mid-sentence, which the rendered changelog showed but
        `git log` did not. Fixed by reflowing every footer onto ONE line, which
        is immune to it regardless of content.

    cliff.toml now renders the footer text too, as a lazy markdown continuation
    of the commit's bullet - `trim = true` strips leading whitespace, so a
    nested list item is not available, and the template's own indentation
    depends on that trim. So the changelog says what to change, not just that
    something changed. Verified with git-cliff 2.13.1: 12 commits carry
    [**breaking**] and all 12 descriptions now parse in full.


================================================================================
PHASE 7g - the leftovers, after Luke's clarifications                       DONE
================================================================================

Three loose ends, all closed. The crate now has ZERO ignored tests.

1. bus::test - 9 ignored stubs, now implemented.
   All nine were bare `todo!()` bodies with a comment naming the registers they
   were meant to cover, so they would have panicked if run. Nothing else covers
   bus-level register ROUTING - the mapper tests cover board internals, the PPU
   and APU tests cover those components directly, but which address reaches
   which component was untested. Implemented all nine against what the routing
   actually does:
     - read_write_ppu: $2000-$2007 and their mirrors up to $3FFF, write-only
       registers reading back PPU open bus, and $2002's vblank clear happening
       on read but NOT on peek.
     - read_write_apu: the $4017 asymmetry - WRITING it is the APU frame
       counter, READING it is controller two. Getting that backwards is silent.
     - write_apu_pulse/triangle/noise/dmc: each block reaches its own channel
       and leaves the others alone, plus the unmapped holes ($4009, $400D) doing
       nothing, and the DMC's scaled $4012/$4013 encodings.
     - read_write_input: $4016 strobe, and both pads shifting out independently.
     - read_write_mapper: writes >= $4100 reach the board, writes below it do
       not.
     - reset: hard clears WRAM, soft preserves it.
   Negative-tested: misrouting the $4017 write, dropping the PPU mirror mask,
   and pointing pulse 2's timer at pulse 1 each fail exactly one test.

   Two of the nine failed first time on MY expectations, not on bugs:
   LengthCounter::write sets `reload`, not `counter` (the frame counter loads it
   later), and an 8K CHR cart has only one bank so a bank switch is invisible.

2. phase_reset.nes / volumes.nes - REMOVED, behaviour asserted directly.
   Neither has any pass/fail output. volumes.txt is explicit that it is a
   listening test: you compare relative channel volumes against reference
   recordings in a `recordings/` folder we do not ship, and it needs A pressed
   on controller 1 for its second half. phase_reset is a tone you judge by ear.
   No snapshot of either could have meant anything.
   What they demonstrate is now asserted with no ROM and no listener:
     - apu::pulse::tests::writing_timer_hi_resets_the_duty_phase_but_not_the_divider
       ($4003/$4007 restart the duty sequencer but leave the divider running)
     - apu::tests::the_mixer_is_non_linear (the resistor-ladder mixer compresses:
       every step is smaller than the last, and silence is truly silent)
   Strictly better than the ROMs - checkable, and they name what broke.

3. zapper_flip, per Luke: it does BOTH - hides the white square when triggered
   AND plays audio while the aim is over the square. So its light-sensing half
   overlaps zapper_light entirely. Neither ROM is ours and it is not clear why
   both exist. Left as-is: only zapper_flip's screen half is asserted, so the
   audio half is covered once (by zapper_light) rather than twice.
